  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1033 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nHot and humid conditions continue as the Mid-South is trapped\nunder a 594 dam ridge as of Thursday evening. The ridge will\ncontinue to retrograde into Friday for our hottest day of the\nweek thus far with air temperatures in the upper 90s to lower\n100s. Shear profiles remain lackluster under this ridge, so\nsingle cell showers and thunderstorms could occur Friday\nafternoon within the diurnal cumulus field. However, this will\nnot aid in much relief from the heat. Storms throughout this\nperiod are not likely to threaten severe weather.\n\nThis pattern is anticipated to continue throughout the weekend\nwith an Extreme Heat Warning and Heat Advisory in effect through\n8 PM Sunday. By Sunday evening, return flow finally eases and a\nmore zonal to northwest flow regime will emerge. This will knock\ntemperatures down a few degrees, but more importantly, filter in\nslightly drier air reducing heat index values.\n\nDespite the northwest flow, probabilities from the NBM are nil\nfor any mentionable rainfall through the forecast period, which\ncould lead to rapid onset drought conditions. To increase\nconfidence on the possibility of rapid onset drought, long term\nguidance favors another ridge sliding east late next week with\nthe return of southerly flow. Heat headlines are uncertain for\nearly into the work week, but could very easily make another\nappearance late next week if these trends continue. For now,\ncontinue to practice heat safety and prepare for another hot\nweek.\n\nDNM",
